    "tags": ["Wearable", "Input", "Touchscreen", "Consumer", "Pocket Computer"],
    "overview": "The Casio VDB-1000 (module 658) is a 1991 touchscreen Databank watch from Casio Computer Co., part of a line of early 'smartwatch' experiments. Where earlier Databank watches used physical buttons or touch keys, the VDB-1000's entire watch face is a graphic display with a touchscreen — you tap characters directly on the glass to enter text, navigate menus, and use the calculator. It is effectively a finger-touch personal data bank worn on the wrist, years before smartphone interaction patterns were common.\n\nIt belongs to the same family of early Casio touch wrist devices (VDB-200, the kanji DKW-100) that Casio built through the 1980s–90s, which The Verge called 'the original smartwatches.' The VDB-1000 is notable for pushing text entry onto a small wearable touch surface — a genuinely strange and prescient interaction for 1991, sitting just inside the museum's 1976–1992 window. It is distinct from the museum's Seiko RC-1000 (which uses a data-entry keypad), and from EyeTap (which mediates vision rather than offering touch text entry).",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "A touchscreen on your wrist",
        "text": "The VDB-1000's defining feature is that the watch face itself is the input surface — a graphic LCD with a touch layer you tap to type and select. In 1991, direct finger-touch text entry on a wearable was an unusual, forward-looking interaction. It is the same gesture vocabulary a smartphone uses, a decade before mainstream touch UIs, compressed onto a wrist-worn pocket data bank."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The Databank as personal memory",
        "text": "Casio's Databank line treated the watch as a personal data-storage device — phone numbers, schedules, notes. The VDB-1000 made that data editable by touch, not by pecking at tiny buttons. It sits in the museum's organizer/memory-family lineage (Sharp Wizard, Psion Organiser, Toshiba LC-836MN) but as a wearable whose text entry is direct touch rather than a keyboard or keypad."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Wearable touch before the phone",
        "text": "The VDB-1000 is a commercial, wearable rehearsal for the touchscreen interaction that would define a later generation of devices. Its strangeness is precisely its point: a tiny glass square you tap, strapped to the wrist, in 1991. It is one of the earliest commercial touchscreen text-entry wearables, image-documented on Commons."
      }
